WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.263
Strong DFIR work is driven by questions.

00:00:03.263 --> 00:00:06.910
The first question is usually simple: what happened?

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:17.083
The answer may start as a rough description, such as a suspicious login, encrypted files, abnormal outbound traffic, or a user report of mailbox activity.

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:21.114
As evidence improves, the description should become more precise.

00:00:21.114 --> 00:00:26.680
A vague alert becomes a timeline of actions, systems, accounts, tools, and decisions.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.943
The next question is when it happened.

00:00:29.943 --> 00:00:32.054
Timing helps define scope.

00:00:32.054 --> 00:00:42.611
Analysts may need to identify the first known suspicious event, the first confirmed malicious action, the time of containment, and any gaps where evidence is missing.

00:00:42.611 --> 00:00:48.561
Time zones, clock drift, log retention, and delayed ingestion can all complicate this work.

00:00:48.561 --> 00:00:52.592
A careful timeline helps the team avoid false certainty.

00:00:52.592 --> 00:00:57.390
Scope is another core question: what systems and accounts were affected?

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:04.876
A compromised account may touch cloud storage, email, collaboration platforms, VPN, source code, administrative consoles, and business applications.

00:01:04.876 --> 00:01:10.442
A compromised endpoint may lead to lateral movement, credential theft, or malware deployment.

00:01:10.442 --> 00:01:17.160
DFIR teams look for connections between artifacts rather than treating each alert as an isolated event.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:27.333
Investigators also ask how the activity started, what data or services were impacted, whether the threat is still active, and what must be done next.

00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:30.212
These questions connect analysis to action.

00:01:30.212 --> 00:01:36.547
If the initial vector was phishing, the response may include mailbox searches and user notification.

00:01:36.547 --> 00:01:42.113
If an exposed service was exploited, patching and external scanning may become urgent.

00:01:42.113 --> 00:01:48.447
If data was accessed, legal and privacy teams may need a defensible record of facts.

00:01:48.447 --> 00:01:53.629
The investigation is successful when it helps the organization make safe decisions.
